WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
Runtime hardening is about reducing what a container can access or change when it runs.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:09.220
A practical starting point is running as a non-root user where possible,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.680
because root inside a container can increase impact when paired with weak runtime

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.220
configuration or host exposure.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.720
Container user identity should be intentional, documented,

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.460
and compatible with the application’s filesystem and process needs.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.640
Least privilege also means dropping unnecessary capabilities, limiting writable paths,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.900
using read-only filesystem concepts when practical,

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:34.840
and avoiding broad host namespace exposure.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.980
Security profiles at a high level can restrict system behavior.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.520
Resource limits help reduce the impact of runaway or abusive workloads on shared

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.780
infrastructure.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.400
These controls are most useful when they become defaults rather than special hardening

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.540
applied only to a few workloads.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.400
Privileged containers are high-risk because they may grant broad access to host-level

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.100
capabilities or sensitive system functions.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.720
Host path mounts also require care.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.980
Mounting the container runtime socket, system directories,

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.200
or sensitive host paths can blur the boundary between container and host.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:14.700
Some workloads legitimately need deeper host access, but those exceptions should be rare,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.320
documented, and reviewed.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:21.380
Hardening should be tested with the application, not guessed into place at the end.

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.280
Teams need to know which files must be writable, which ports are needed,

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.420
which processes are expected, which capabilities are required,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.640
and what resource limits are realistic.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.660
The secure target is not an unusable container.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.280
It is a container that has enough access to perform its purpose and little else.
